 The existing block occurrence owns the query. Reject revoked/stale scopes, invalid mounted roots/shells and recursive text queries before schema or `toText` callbacks. Staged candidates may read their own prospective record before publication; discarded candidates never retain query authority. Run all schema mapping, decoding, snapshot capture and text callbacks under the existing protected extension-preparation boundary. Nested mutation, control and task acquisition must fail before their producer runs. Revalidate the same scope epoch/generation and block owner after callbacks; adoption or revocation cannot bless a new owner. No deferred authority is returned.
 
+Scoped block `getData()` snapshots use this same existing block-owner check before reading their record and after snapshot capture. Staged construction, prospective updates/recovery and ordinary read-only access remain supported; an adopted root or shell cannot use its old context to obtain a fresh snapshot for an asynchronous view update. This read does not opt into field-text updates, add a public capability or retire an owner through a new path. Provider-driven presentation must report a failed read once without attempting that rejected view update again from its error handler. Native rich authoring remains owned by the core input settlement; an independent deferred blur notification is not new authority over an adopted block.
+
 A successful query opts that existing block lifetime into ordinary `update(next, previous)` delivery when its canonical inline sidecars change, including when `next` and `previous` block data are equal. Data changes keep their existing delivery; a consumer without optional `update` uses the existing replacement path. Do not introduce another lifecycle hook, event bridge, registry, dependency cache or canonical store. An unchanged record and healthy repeated field queries do not manufacture updates. Native retained-source projection keeps its existing author-DOM/caret admission and section 16.7 completion, rather than calling the block's update over pending native input. All derived text must settle inside the operation's existing fallible pre-commit projection, never its post-commit finalizer.
 
 Query/formatter failures preserve every thrown value and use current construction, canonical/history, mode or caller recovery owners. Callable thenables and promises are rejected synchronously; no background completion is scheduled. A read failure does not become a document edit or a new failure/retry API. Auxiliary writes remain the block's responsibility and require its original still-owned nodes. The opt-in does not authorize remounting an unrelated active author field or changing canonical/history/selection state for a label.
